Here is the revised speaker text for slides 12 through 20, crafted entirely in a continuous, natural spoken style with smooth transitions from one slide to the next.
Slide 12: Make the Agent Initialize Before Every Work Session
 * Speaker Text: "As we dive into structuring our workflow, we first need to look at how we kick things off. Initialization and implementation have completely different optimization targets. Implementation wants to write feature code, while initialization builds the infrastructure—like setting up a runnable environment, test frameworks, and task checklists. If you mix them, the agent sacrifices infrastructure to write code faster, causing reliability to suffer later. We must enforce initialization first. For example, before an agent starts coding a new login page, the initialization phase automatically runs a script that provisions a clean database instance, checks out a specific feature branch, and generates a structured task checklist."
Slide 13: Draw Clear Task Boundaries for Agents
 * Speaker Text: "Building on that proper setup, we also have to control how much work the agent takes on at once. Agents suffer when given too many tasks at once because context capacity and reasoning resources are finite. That’s why WIP=1 is the safest default for agent harnesses. We want only one task active at any time, ensuring the agent focuses fully on getting one specific behavior verification to pass before moving on. For instance, instead of telling the agent to implement user authentication, password reset, and profile management all at once, the harness restricts it to picking only user authentication from the queue until it fully passes verification."
Slide 14: Use Feature Lists to Constrain What the Agent Does
 * Speaker Text: "Now, to keep that single active task strictly constrained, we need to define what 'done' actually looks like. Agents don’t automatically know what we mean by 'done'—without a clear rule, they rely on subjective standards like 'there are no syntax errors.' To prevent this, feature state must live in a machine-readable file right inside the repository, tracking behavior definitions, verification commands, and operational states. A great example of this is using a features.json file where an item explicitly specifies its behavior, such as POST /cart/items returns 201, along with its verification command and state."
Slide 15: Feature Lists as System Primitives
 * Speaker Text: "Those feature lists aren't just reference guides; they serve as core system primitives. Remember: documents are for humans to read, but primitives are for systems to execute. Feature lists act as database-level constraints that components like the scheduler, verifier, and handoff reporter depend on to automate the entire development lifecycle safely. For example, the harness scheduler reads features.json, automatically picks the next item marked as not started, executes its verification command, and updates its state without requiring human intervention."
Slide 16: Prevent Agents from Declaring Victory Too Early
 * Speaker Text: "Because the system automates this check, we can actively prevent the agent from jumping the gun. Agents are systematically overconfident, and just because code is written doesn't mean it works correctly. The harness must replace the agent's internal 'feelings' of success with externalized, execution-based verification. We cannot let 'the code looks fine' become evidence that a feature is complete. So, even if the agent claims it is finished writing the feature, the harness overrides it, runs the test suite, executes startup checks, and blocks completion until all automated assertions pass successfully."
Slide 17: Three-Layer Termination Check
 * Speaker Text: "To ensure that verification is bulletproof, we implement a comprehensive checking mechanism. To ensure true completion, we rely on a three-layer termination check. Layer 1 handles low-cost syntax and static analysis. Layer 2 verifies runtime behavior through tests. Layer 3 provides system-level confirmation like end-to-end simulation. Skipping these layers leaves configuration, database, or interface bugs completely hidden. For example, a feature might pass unit tests in Layer 2, but the harness runs a full integration script simulating a user journey in Layer 3, catching a hidden environment dependency error before the session closes."
Slide 18: Making the Agent's Runtime Observable
 * Speaker Text: "To understand why any of those layers pass or fail, we must keep a close eye on execution. Without observability, agents make decisions under uncertainty, turning retries into blind wandering. Code review shows us what was written, but runtime tracing shows us what actually ran. Exposing these signals prevents information cliffs and saves valuable session time. For example, the harness logs all network requests, health checks, and trace outputs during agent execution, allowing developers to immediately inspect why a test failed instead of guessing blindly."
Slide 19: Leave a Clean Handoff at the End of Every Session
 * Speaker Text: "All of this observability and verification leads us smoothly into wrapping up our work sessions cleanly. Long-term reliability depends on operational discipline, because entropy growth is the default state of messy codebases. A clean state is much more than just 'the code compiles'—it means builds pass, tests pass, progress is recorded, and there are no stale artifacts left behind. For instance, at the end of a session, a teardown script automatically purges temporary debug logs, verifies that the standard application startup path works smoothly, and updates the progress tracker file."
Slide 20: Clean State, Cleanup Loops, and Harness Simplification
 * Speaker Text: "Finally, to sustain that operational discipline over the long haul, we need systematic maintenance loops. To prevent entropy from compounding over multiple sessions, we need regular maintenance loops and idempotent cleanups. As models improve, we must also periodically simplify our harnesses by removing redundant components that are no longer necessary. A practical way to do this is setting up a weekly cleanup routine that deletes orphaned temporary test files, rolls back messy experimental scripts, and streamlines the harness configuration files to ensure the next session starts fresh."
